---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
import { affiliates } from '../../data/affiliates';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Frase Review (2026): Pricing, GEO Features, and Who It’s For"
  description="Frase review for 2026: Jan rebuild, Starter/Pro/Scale pricing, GEO/AI visibility, and who should skip it. Dated Aug 14, 2026—verify live."
  moneyPage
>
  <AffiliateDisclosure />
  <article class="prose-spl mt-6 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">August 14, 2026 · Research review</p>
    <h1 class="!mt-2 text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Frase Review: Is the Rebuilt SEO + GEO Platform Worth It?</h1>
    <p>
      <a href={affiliates.frase.url} rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Frase</a>
      is no longer easiest to describe as a cheaper content optimizer. After a January 2026 rebuild, the company
      positions it as an SEO + GEO (AI-visibility) content platform: research, briefs, drafts, dual scoring, and
      monitoring in one loop. This is a research-based review of that positioning, dated public pricing, and who
      should buy or skip—not a fabricated multi-month diary, ranking screenshot, or income claim. Pricing notes are
      as of August 14, 2026; the live
      <a href="https://www.frase.io/pricing">frase.io/pricing</a>
      page should be checked on purchase day (it can time out or rebundle). AI-assisted drafting helped structure
      this article.
    </p>

    <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">Quick pick</p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Need briefs plus SEO/GEO in one loop → try Frase. Need a score-heavy editor on already-briefed drafts → see Surfer vs Frase, not this review.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href={affiliates.frase.url} class="btn-primary" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Check Frase pricing</a>
        <a href="/blog/frase-vs-surfer-2026" class="btn-secondary">Frase vs Surfer</a>
        <a href="/blog/is-surfer-seo-worth-it-2026" class="btn-secondary">Is Surfer worth it?</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>The January 2026 rebuild: not “cheap Surfer”</h2>
    <p>
      Frase’s own January 2026 launch posts describe a ground-up rebuild, not a skin on the old brief tool. Vendor
      language now leads with AI visibility as a first-class job beside classic search: dual SEO and GEO scoring,
      citation-style tracking across AI answer engines, and a research → write → publish → monitor loop. Treat that
      as positioning, not a proof that Frase “wins AI search.”
    </p>
    <p>
      The useful review question is whether you will run that loop weekly. If you only wanted a cheaper content score
      than
      <a href={affiliates.surfer.url} rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Surfer SEO</a>,
      you are shopping the wrong frame—and you should read
      <a href="/blog/frase-vs-surfer-2026">Frase vs Surfer (2026)</a>
      instead of this page. If you wanted a brief-and-coverage tool that also watches GEO, the rebuild is why Frase
      is still in the conversation.
    </p>

    <h2>Official prices (August 14, 2026—verify live)</h2>
    <p>
      Self-serve list prices from Frase’s pricing page notes as of August 14, 2026 (annual typically ~20% off). Do
      not buy from a screenshot, and do not use retired packaging such as a historic ~$15 Solo plan.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Starter:</strong> $49/mo, or $39/mo billed annually. One seat. Hard stop at monthly limits (third-party roundups often cite ~10 articles/mo—confirm the live meter).</li>
      <li><strong>Professional:</strong> $129/mo, or $103/mo annual. Three seats. Optional pay-as-you-go overages; extra seats commonly ~$29/mo.</li>
      <li><strong>Scale:</strong> $299/mo, or $239/mo annual. More seats/volume; extra seats ~$29/mo on Pro/Scale.</li>
      <li><strong>Enterprise:</strong> custom. Confirm SSO, white-label, and volume with sales.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Prefer monthly until a 7-day trial proves weekly use. Match article, audit, and seat meters to the next 30 days
      of real output—not a hoped-for calendar.
    </p>

    <h2>GEO / AI visibility: what is claimed vs what you should verify</h2>
    <p>
      Frase markets GEO scoring (citation-readiness alongside on-page SEO) and AI-visibility tracking across engines
      such as ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, Gemini, and Google AI surfaces. Homepage and feature pages describe dual
      scores on the same draft and monitoring as part of the loop, not a separate “AI add-on” tab.
    </p>
    <p>
      Self-serve plans are sold as the same research → write → SEO/GEO loop; seats and monthly meters differ. Do not
      assume a specific GEO dashboard, prompt-tracking cap, or engine list is unlocked on Starter versus Scale unless
      the live pricing grid says so. Confirm gated meters on
      <a href="https://www.frase.io/pricing">frase.io/pricing</a>
      the day you subscribe. Vendor GEO scores are not a ranking or citation guarantee.
    </p>

    <h2>Trial: no-card 7 days vs Surfer’s card-required Pro trial</h2>
    <p>
      Frase commonly advertises a 7-day trial with no credit card. Surfer’s pricing FAQ commonly notes a 7-day Pro
      trial that typically requires a card. That difference matters if you want to test the category without a hold.
      It is not a reason to skip the
      <a href="/blog/is-surfer-seo-worth-it-2026">Is Surfer SEO worth it?</a>
      question if in-editor scoring is already your weekly bottleneck.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the Frase trial to answer two things: will someone open briefs or GEO/SEO scores the same week you publish,
      and do Starter limits cover that week? If the trial sits idle, do not convert to annual.
    </p>

    <h2>Best for / Skip if</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Best for:</strong> SEO content ops whose bottleneck is keyword → brief → draft coverage, and teams that
      explicitly want GEO/AI-visibility tracking in the same product as briefs—not a second “AI search” seat.
      <strong>Skip if:</strong> you already have excellent briefs and only need a score-heavy editor (start at
      <a href="/blog/frase-vs-surfer-2026">Frase vs Surfer</a>);
      you need a full keyword-research and site-audit suite first (Semrush-class tools); nobody will maintain a
      briefing or monitoring workflow weekly; or Starter article caps cannot cover your calendar and you will not pay
      Professional.
    </p>

    <h2>Practical pick</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Briefs and topic coverage (and optionally GEO) are the weekly job → run the no-card Frase trial; Starter if meters match volume.</li>
      <li>Drafts already exist and competitive on-page scoring is the job → do not use this review as a Surfer substitute; read Frase vs Surfer and Is Surfer worth it.</li>
      <li>You only publish occasionally → stay on ChatGPT/Claude + Search Console; an optimizer will not create a calendar.</li>
      <li>Trial unused after seven days → do not buy annual “in case GEO matters later.”</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Stack mapping (research → draft → optimize) lives in
      <a href="/blog/ai-content-ops-stack-research-draft-optimize">How to Build an AI Content Ops Stack</a>.
      Budget path if Frase is the wrong category:
      <a href="/blog/best-surfer-seo-alternatives-budget">Surfer alternatives on a budget</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Related reading:</strong>
      <a href="/blog/frase-vs-surfer-2026">Frase vs Surfer (2026)</a>,
      <a href="/blog/is-surfer-seo-worth-it-2026">Is Surfer SEO worth it?</a>,
      <a href="/blog/best-surfer-seo-alternatives-budget">Surfer alternatives</a>,
      <a href="/blog/ai-content-ops-stack-research-draft-optimize">content ops stack</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
